<Docs edit="site/src/pages/docs/concepts/design-tokens.astro" title="Design tokens" description="The named values every colour, size, gap and movement in Facet comes from." group="Concepts" current={url('/docs/concepts/design-tokens/')} headings={headings}>
  <h2 id="what">What a token is</h2>
  <p>
    A design token is a named value: the surface colour, the gap between cards, the length of a press animation.
    Components never use a raw number; they ask for a token. Change the token and every component that uses it
    follows, on every screen.
  </p>
  <p>
    The tokens live in one JSON file per foundation in the repository (<code>renderer/tokens/</code>). The build turns
    them into the C++ the panel runs and into the pages of this site, so the docs and the panel never disagree.
  </p>

  <h2 id="kinds">The kinds of token</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Foundation</th><th>What it holds</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {kinds.map(([name, id, about]) => <tr><td><a href={url(`/foundations/${id}/`)}>{name}</a></td><td>{about}</td></tr>)}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Sound is a foundation too: each component names the sound it plays, and the sound pack decides how it sounds. The
    <a href={url('/foundations/')}>Design system</a> section has a page for each, with the values drawn live.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rules">Rules every value follows</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>4 px grid</strong>: every size and gap is a whole number of 4 px steps.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Contrast</strong>: every place two colours meet is listed and tested against the WCAG AA levels, in both
      themes and with every accent colour, on the 16-bit colours the panel really shows. Facet ships with no failures.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="sizes">One layout, two sizes</h2>
  <p>
    The 480 x 480 panel is the reference size. The 720 x 720 panel uses the same layout at 1.5 times the size, so a
    screen looks the same on both, just sharper on the bigger one. The size switch at the top of each page shows either.
  </p>

  <h2 id="panel">Made for the panel</h2>
  <p>
    The panels show 16-bit colour, which has fewer shades than a computer screen. Facet's colours are chosen for that:
    softer looks come from a less saturated colour, not from transparency, and gradients are dithered so they do not
    band. What you see on these pages is drawn the same way.
  </p>
</Docs>
